\section{Problem statement and classification}
There are $n$ lectures repeating every day. Lecture $i$ is a circular arc
$[s_i,e_i)$ of a circle of length $L=1440$ minutes and may wrap past midnight.
Two lectures \emph{overlap} if they share positive length (touching endpoints
do not overlap). Find $S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$ such that every lecture
overlaps \emph{exactly one} lecture of $S$ (a lecture never counts itself).

\paragraph{Classification.}
Let $G$ be the overlap graph (a \emph{circular-arc graph}) and $\N(v)$ the open
neighbourhood of $v$. We need $S$ with $|\N(v)\cap S|=1$ for all $v$: an
\emph{efficient open dominating set} (total perfect code). Technique: structural
characterisation + dynamic programming on a circular sequence (linearised by
anchoring), with bit-set graph operations. Exhaustive search would take
$O(2^n)$; the algorithm below takes $O(n^3)$.

