\section{Correctness}
\begin{theorem}
\textsc{SelectLectures} returns a valid subset if one exists and \textsc{None}
otherwise.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Soundness.} A returned chain consists of blocks, so each region has
$\N(a)\cap\N(b)=\emptyset$. Regions are disjoint by the condition $x_e\le y_s$
and by restricting candidates to $[\ell_0,L]$ (C1). Suppose a lecture $w$
overlaps two regions. The lecture is a contiguous arc, so it contains the whole
gap between two cyclically consecutive regions $X,Y$ and meets both; then some
copy of $w$ starts before $x_e$ and ends after $y_s$, giving $Q(x_e)>y_s$ and
contradicting the transition test (C2). If $w$ meets no region it lies strictly
inside one gap, so $T(x_e)\le y_s$, again contradicted (C3). Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp}
finishes.

\emph{Completeness.} Take any valid $S$ with blocks $B_1,\dots,B_k$ in cyclic
order. By Lemma~\ref{lem:decomp} exactly one block meets lecture~1; the loop
tries it as the anchor. Conditions (C1)--(C3) translate, by the argument above,
into the transition and closing tests, so the DP reaches $B_k$ and closes.
The special case of a block covering the whole circle ($k=1$) is checked
directly: its validity needs only $\N(a)\cap\N(b)=\emptyset$.
\end{proof}

